# Rocdown blocks

Ordinary prose stays Markdown. A line-start `:note` is a block; a colon in a
sentence is not. See also [[Guide]] and [[Interactive]].

Content is the rest of the line, a `{{ }}` section, or a matching `:end.kind`.

:note Do not paste raw HTML into Markdown. Use a block when the page needs structure beyond CommonMark.

:caution[title: "Removed spelling"] Leftover `@docs caution` is a removal error. Write `:caution` instead.

:tip Line-scope still works when the body fits on one line.

:note[title: "Static pages"] {{
    Rocdown accepts these blocks next to Markdown and `@page`.

    `@render`, `@roc`, and other islands still require `rocdown run` / `rocci run`.
}}

## Procedures and alternatives

:steps
    :step[title: "Write a block"] Start a document-root line with `:` and the kind name.
    :step[title: "Prefer the rest of the line"] One-line content does not need braces.
    :step[title: "Wrap nested document"] {{
        Use `{{ ... }}` when the body has paragraphs, fences, or nested blocks.
    }}
:end.steps

:tabs[group: "os", kind: "platform"]
    :tab[id: "mac", label: "macOS"] Mac panel.
    :tab[id: "linux", label: "Linux"] Linux panel.
:end.tabs

## Figures, labels, and extra structure

:figure[caption: "A banana", credit: "Rocci fixtures"] {{
    :img[src: "./img/yammi_banana.png", alt: "A banana", width: "50px"]
}}

:badge[label: "preview", tone: "preview"]

:details[summary: "What stays in the document"] {{
    Every tab panel and disclosure body is in the HTML. Print and browsers
    with scripts disabled still show the content.
}}

:definition[term: "Article block"] A `:kind[params]` node with optional Markdown or nested blocks as its body.
